From 82e7a2f003ac7c57e78af3f6d7b7269c9bc39b5f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: abulxt-local <local@abulxt.invalid>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 19:52:05 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 4/4] wisp: ingress window that tracks what the upstream has
 actually drained

The upload mirror of the previous patch. submitIngress closed a stream with
0x49 (THROTTLED) when its per-stream ingress queue would exceed
PendingQueueSize (64 MiB), and nothing ever slowed the client down first:
handleDataPacket decremented the client's CONTINUE budget per packet but
refilled it to BufferRemainingLength (32768 packets) unconditionally the
moment it reached zero, and 32768 packets of up to maxPayloadSize is not a
meaningful window anyway. So a client that can push into the relay faster
than the relay can deliver upstream (a browser on a local relay with a
slower uplink; any slow target) overran the queue in under a second and lost
the stream mid-upload.

Captured with a raw Wisp v1 client, 4 parallel 200 MiB POSTs to a loopback
sink reading at 5 MB/s: every stream was closed by the relay with 0x49 at
~133 MB sent, with zero CONTINUE packets ever sent.

ingressWindow() bounds the advertised window so a full window of
maximum-size packets fits in half the queue:
min(BufferRemainingLength, PendingQueueSize/2/maxPayloadSize), 128 by
default. It is what the initial stream-0 CONTINUE (v1 and v2) and the
stream-confirm CONTINUE now carry. The budget is replenished only as the
ingress writer actually delivers upstream: submitIngress counts packets in
flight and the budget the client believes it has (packets buffered before
CONNECT completes are counted too, since the client spent budget on them),
and ingressDrained sends CONTINUE(window - inflight) once at least half a
window has been freed since the last announcement, or whenever the queue has
fully drained while the client holds less than the whole window. The 0x49
cap stays as a backstop for clients that ignore CONTINUE. The UDP path keeps
its direct write and simple refill.

wisp/ingress_flowcontrol_test.go: no CONTINUE while the upstream is stalled,
the whole window handed back once it drains (twice, no drift), and packets
sent before CONNECT completes are counted so the client is never left
blocked on an empty queue.

Verified through the relay: the same 4x200 MiB
uploads at 5 MB/s complete exactly (128 CONTINUEs each), 256 KiB-packet
uploads at 20 MB/s complete, fast-sink uploads unchanged, downloads
unchanged.
